David Hairston writes:
>[leonard@mimsy.umd.edu (Leonard Dickens ) writes:]
>[] How often have I wanted to teleport right next to a juicy wraith
>[] on the other side of the screen?  It would be so much faster to type
>[]	"^tWj."   than  "^thhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhkkkhh."
>[] Doesn't look *that* difficult to program, either.  Dev team?
>
>there's no doubt that the teleport interface could use some treatment
>but a lot of the current suggestions have made assumptions that give
>away something for nothing (whether that's good or bad is another
>issue).  i think Dave Cohrs had it right, namely you should only be
>able to teleport to stairs that you've already traversed.  anything
>other than this gives away info.  
Not true, *if* you require that the player still has to verify where he/she
is going to land via hitting ".", as is currently the case.  What happens 
when you hit ">" is that the program will look at the [data structure repre-
senting what is on the] screen, find the first ">" that is printed there, and
move your cursor on top of it.  Note that there is *not* an assumption here
that the ">" is actually a staircase -- or that it is actually anything.  Just
that it is printed on the screen -- i.e., it could be a mimic, a real stair,
or an old hallucination.  Also, if you have not found anything that looks like
">", then you could not use ">" to go anywhere.  No information is given away.

>along those same lines, you could
>attach the "verified" bit to other dungeon architectures (i.e. altars,
>etc.), and be able to directly teleport to them also, if you've already
>verified what they are!  teleporting directly to monsters and objects
>is, at best, idiosyncratic ...

A verified bit is just extra overhead that is not necessary.  Kludgy, IMO.
In regard to teleporting to monsters, how many times have you ever teleported 
to a place on the screen that was not near any distinctive landmark?  Most 
often I am teleporting to stairs, near monsters, or to altars.  Very rarely do
I teleport to a place that is near nothing (in order to heal, for instance).
Why not do it right the first time through?  The code would look something
like this:


--------------------------------------------------------------------------

#define SCREEN_X 79
#define SCREEN_Y 23

extern char screen[SCREEN_X][SCREEN_Y] ;    
/* An array holding the actual characters printed on the screen that the 
   player is currently looking at. */

static int CursorX, CursorY ;  /* Where the cursor is now. */


void StartGuidedPort(PlayerStruct *player)
{
  CursorX = player->px ;
  CursorY = player->py ;
}

void MovePortCursor(char incoming)
{
  int sweepX, SweepY ;

  switch (incoming) {
  case '.': 
    /* Do whatever is necessary to end TPort. */
    return ;
  case 'h': CursorX-- ;  if (CursorX < 0)  CursorX = 0 ;       return ;
  case 'l': CursorX++ ;  if (CursorX > SCREEN_X)  CursorX = SCREEN_X ;
       return ;
  case 'j': CursorY++ ;  if (CursorY > SCREEN_Y)  CursorY = SCREEN_Y ;
       return ;
  case 'k': CursorY-- ;  if (CursorY < 0)  CursorX = 0 ;       return ;
  /* Etc for other cursor motion commands... */

  /* Search the screen for the next char like the incoming char: */
  default:
    sweepX = CursorX ;
    sweepY = CursorY ;
    do {
      sweepX++ ;
      if (sweepX > SCREEN_X)
        sweepY ++, sweepX = 0 ;
      if (sweepY > SCREEN_Y)
        sweepY = 0 ;
      if ((sweepX == CursorX) && (sweepY == CursorY))
	{
	/* Abnormal: no such character found on the screen.  
		Do whatever is right, probably beep and not move. */
	return ;
	}
      }
    while (screen[sweepX][sweepY] != incoming)
    CursorX = sweepX ;
    CursorY = sweepY ;
  }
}





